      "question": "Five vials hold quantum dots: tiny crystals of cadmium selenide, a few nanometers (nm) across. The material is identical in every vial. The 2 nm dots glow blue and the 6 nm dots glow red. Why would size alone set the color?",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "The smaller the crystal, the more tightly its electrons are squeezed. That raises their energy and shifts the light toward blue.",
