      "options": [
        {
          "text": "B = 0, so only the sine term survives",
          "isCorrect": true,
          "reason": "At x = 0, sin 0 = 0 and cos 0 = 1, so ψ(0) = B. Setting that to 0 forces B = 0 and leaves A sin kx.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "A = 0, so only the cosine term survives, since cos is largest at x = 0",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "It's tempting to think cos is the natural wall-hugging function. But cos 0 = 1 means the cosine term contributes B at x = 0, so B has to be 0, not A.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "A = 0 and B = 0, because a zero at the wall must kill both terms",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "Reading \"ψ = 0\" as \"everything is 0\" would leave no particle. Here sin 0 = 0 already, so A is untouched and only B is forced to 0.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "k = 0, because the wall sits at x = 0",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "This mixes up the position x = 0 with the wavenumber k. The condition at x = 0 fixes B. It doesn't touch k yet.",
