      "image": null,
      "visual": null,
      "basis": {
        "screen": 2,
        "quote": "Since ψ has no jumps, it must reach 0 at the walls: ψ(0) = 0 and ψ(L) = 0."
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "workedExample",
      "title": "Quantizing the energy",
      "problem": "The left wall gave B = 0, so ψ(x) = A sin kx. Apply ψ(L) = 0 at the right wall and find the allowed energies of the box.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "text": "Impose the right wall: ψ(L) = 0 gives A sin kL = 0.",
          "math": "A\\sin kL = 0"
        },
        {
          "text": "A can't be 0, otherwise ψ is zero everywhere and there is no particle. So sin kL = 0.",
          "math": "\\sin kL = 0"
        },
        {
          "text": "A sine vanishes only at whole multiples of π, so kL = nπ. The allowed k values are evenly spaced dots on the k axis.",
          "math": "kL = n\\pi \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k_n = \\frac{n\\pi}{L}"
        },
        {
          "text": "Exclude n = 0: it gives k = 0 and ψ(x) = A sin 0 = 0 everywhere, so there is no particle.",
          "math": "n = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\psi \\equiv 0"
        },
        {
          "text": "Negative n add nothing new: sin(−kx) = −sin kx is the same state up to a sign. So n = 1, 2, 3, …",
          "math": "\\sin(-kx) = -\\sin kx"
        },
        {
          "text": "Solve k = √(2mE)/ħ for E, then substitute k_n.",
          "math": "E_n = \\frac{\\hbar^2 k_n^2}{2m} = \\frac{n^2\\pi^2\\hbar^2}{2mL^2}"
        },
        {
          "text": "Use ħ = h/2π so the π² cancels. The energy then depends on the width as 1/L².",
          "math": "E_n = \\frac{n^2 h^2}{8mL^2}"
        }
      ],
      "result": "Only k_n = nπ/L fit between the walls, so the energy is quantized: only the separate values E_n = n²h²/(8mL²) with n = 1, 2, 3, … are allowed.",
      "image": {
